1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,677
What brings a number back to where it started?

2
00:00:02,677 --> 00:00:06,436
Identities & Inverses. What Brings You Back? Identity means

3
00:00:06,436 --> 00:00:09,495
it changes nothing. Inverse means it brings you back

4
00:00:09,495 --> 00:00:12,809
to a special result. We'll explore this through addition

5
00:00:12,809 --> 00:00:14,211
and multiplication.

6
00:00:14,567 --> 00:00:18,727
Let's build the idea of identity, starting simple. Take

7
00:00:18,727 --> 00:00:23,750
three-fifths, plus zero. Send it through the addition machine.

8
00:00:23,750 --> 00:00:28,459
Out comes three-fifths again — completely unchanged! Adding zero

9
00:00:28,459 --> 00:00:31,677
changes nothing — that's why zero is the additive

10
00:00:31,677 --> 00:00:32,698
identity.

11
00:00:33,050 --> 00:00:38,010
Multiplication has its own do-nothing number too. Three-sevenths, times

12
00:00:38,010 --> 00:00:42,252
one. The result is still three-sevenths! Multiplying by one

13
00:00:42,252 --> 00:00:46,781
changes nothing. That's why one is the multiplicative identity.

14
00:00:47,133 --> 00:00:50,043
Let's compare the two, so we never mix them

15
00:00:50,043 --> 00:00:54,997
up. For addition, the identity is zero. For multiplication,

16
00:00:54,997 --> 00:00:58,929
the identity is one. Remember it simply: add zero,

17
00:00:58,929 --> 00:01:00,344
multiply by one.

18
00:01:00,700 --> 00:01:05,444
Now, something different — the additive inverse. Take three,

19
00:01:05,444 --> 00:01:09,239
and minus three. Watch them come together and cancel

20
00:01:09,239 --> 00:01:13,114
out completely to zero! It works for fractions too:

21
00:01:13,114 --> 00:01:18,332
two-thirds, plus minus two-thirds, collapses straight to zero. Any

22
00:01:18,332 --> 00:01:21,732
number plus its opposite always gives zero — that's

23
00:01:21,732 --> 00:01:23,551
the additive inverse.

24
00:01:23,917 --> 00:01:26,993
Multiplication has its own kind of inverse too —

25
00:01:26,993 --> 00:01:31,376
the reciprocal. What number can multiply with two-sevenths to

26
00:01:31,376 --> 00:01:36,068
make one? Try seven-halves. Multiply them together, and you

27
00:01:36,068 --> 00:01:39,836
get exactly one! Reciprocals always multiply to one —

28
00:01:39,836 --> 00:01:42,528
that's the multiplicative inverse.

29
00:01:42,883 --> 00:01:46,579
But there's one crucial exception — the zero trap.

30
00:01:46,579 --> 00:01:50,675
Can zero, times something, ever equal one? Try zero

31
00:01:50,675 --> 00:01:53,648
times two — that's zero. Try zero times a

32
00:01:53,648 --> 00:01:57,263
hundred — still zero. No matter what you multiply

33
00:01:57,263 --> 00:02:00,395
zero by, you always get zero. So zero has

34
00:02:00,395 --> 00:02:03,850
no multiplicative inverse — one over zero is simply

35
00:02:03,850 --> 00:02:04,974
undefined.

36
00:02:05,333 --> 00:02:08,182
Now it's your turn — figure out identity or

37
00:02:08,182 --> 00:02:12,578
inverse in each one. First, four-fifths plus zero equals

38
00:02:12,578 --> 00:02:19,008
four-fifths. Second, four-fifths plus minus four-fifths equals zero. Third,

39
00:02:19,008 --> 00:02:24,543
five-sixths times six-fifths equals one. Fourth, five-sixths times one

40
00:02:24,543 --> 00:02:29,264
equals five-sixths. Pause the video and classify each one.

41
00:02:40,067 --> 00:02:42,396
The first one added zero and stayed the same

42
00:02:42,396 --> 00:02:45,761
— that's the Additive Identity. The second one cancelled

43
00:02:45,761 --> 00:02:48,607
to zero — that's the Additive Inverse. The third

44
00:02:48,607 --> 00:02:52,101
one multiplied to one — that's the Multiplicative Inverse.

45
00:02:52,101 --> 00:02:54,625
The fourth one multiplied by one and stayed the

46
00:02:54,625 --> 00:02:57,278
same — that's the Multiplicative Identity.

47
00:02:58,283 --> 00:03:02,286
Let's lock in the four rules. Additive identity: a

48
00:03:02,286 --> 00:03:06,790
plus zero equals a. Multiplicative identity: a times one

49
00:03:06,790 --> 00:03:10,709
equals a. Additive inverse: a plus minus a equals

50
00:03:10,709 --> 00:03:15,129
zero. Multiplicative inverse: a times one over a equals

51
00:03:15,129 --> 00:03:20,884
one. Remember: identity changes nothing, inverse brings you back.

52
00:03:20,884 --> 00:03:23,886
Thank you so much for watching, and I will

53
00:03:23,886 --> 00:03:26,054
see you in the next lesson!
